#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175711 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "e4f2ef63-cc21-5aeb-b1c4-6a7a4d40e81a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12411" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eecba17a7af16086022541eaab52f0755f959cb3f0e5059d61322bbbd81c9c4.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$ for $y \\geq 0$ and $z \\leq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/94/9415dd50c82e3f5f4e47e26f459dda1315f819348e87ff97a4b05b1c27b59891.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}, \\: \n{0} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\pi}, \\: \n \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}2} \\leq \\phi \\leq {\\pi}   \n\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "68b95ae7-c0ac-590c-a764-0500455ca70c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12412" :question/problem "![](images/04/0437b193a24fadb4e43ec449e685abd9b8a220ff72019d6c5b1d242e6eb49942.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ for $x \\geq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents a half-sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,-2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho = -4\\cos\\phi.$\n\n![](images/44/449c0a320c59fc072c7ca38307d9929910b1dfa432d7e3afdf11824e97270dc5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\cos\\phi]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq -4\\cos\\phi, \\: \n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: \n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq  \\pi\n\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175816 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "78981c7f-ec42-5eac-afb8-145bd2a7e66c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8811" :question/problem "What is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/a6/a60ddaebcf32cd8d69624b79f34d96d8ca1c18ec59c8bc443a95e235dedfae9e.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\phi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175817 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "bc7cbddf-7c15-5762-b035-cbd68a1f25f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8812" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho=5$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho=5\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $5.$ On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere of radius $5.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a8c82e617e1d040c61f0f7fca8dd35985005df553aaa53b95e4b14871df6d8b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f34521-0fe5-5dba-b5ce-bfd1f7994983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\frac{π}{3}ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173363} {:answer/id #uuid "0c45931a-20a8-5647-b35c-95e54c97ada0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = \\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173364} {:answer/id #uuid "334d3c0a-e4ec-5bc6-8d30-b38115263b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ = \\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173365} {:answer/id #uuid "5080f036-e062-5cfe-b10c-f6140a353ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\frac{π}{3}θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173366} {:answer/id #uuid "738b93f6-729c-5dec-af6c-792776649438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173367}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e03fafa7-5de5-5b87-8159-28addb0e0b3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173362}] :question/id #uuid "52ca5532-94f1-527a-af84-d4e9f2c326d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-102943" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae59540093c131582cf3d7c682a093eea6cbced9d8c88975a925e5c1d71123f9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25770517-4a8b-58a6-aebe-d00c58c307b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236059} {:answer/id #uuid "47637a16-4f57-53c6-a381-c5183d8c2b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =2ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236276} {:answer/id #uuid "524c73d2-e407-5ae0-804d-e9a42101c4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236338} {:answer/id #uuid "ae33c79f-03db-5f7d-9aca-c1041a750e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236924} {:answer/id #uuid "f99c92dd-efb3-508e-8067-3a77809174c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25770517-4a8b-58a6-aebe-d00c58c307b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236059} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa2e7ca-c416-5346-a8a6-bdd2a228a74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f0566ac-a9c9-553f-9d54-910954e3b64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-102949" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e542606b09d564f450aa9c2f4e2de5bf8dc197644a094c37420ee1b881f14e1f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $2.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3393b1f7-6a5f-5ed8-8699-113fb09e64fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =3\\rho " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266596} {:answer/id #uuid "438b6d53-0463-5cb5-be14-303ecf520e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266748} {:answer/id #uuid "aa516819-50e0-5f63-8a35-e7eab0c258c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267683} {:answer/id #uuid "e288cb29-8e2c-5349-a21d-f6d13deaa8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268145} {:answer/id #uuid "e83396d8-4c0d-56db-bda1-c9d30f58b101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =9\\rho " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e288cb29-8e2c-5349-a21d-f6d13deaa8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268145} :answer-field/id #uuid "965d8e4a-1bae-59f6-ac0c-fcb414500ba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cc5ea4-9288-5e4d-b708-8718d21e8b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-135247" :question/problem "![](images/db/db4700a08fd81ab6d2bc07d80d72fb1fc9edea6c0cdf652b251a755b53a2b148.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $3.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f251965-fc78-59a5-84e4-de545ef05e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266414} {:answer/id #uuid "48261a0e-dcbe-5690-861d-4415470d5467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266789} {:answer/id #uuid "76c2155d-f7dc-5291-9fbd-a97616a5e56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267186} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3ae1a7-9ccb-5689-9542-21fd7fd44e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267569} {:answer/id #uuid "de07bfc7-2d59-5f36-b8fd-e6688190600f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c2155d-f7dc-5291-9fbd-a97616a5e56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267186} :answer-field/id #uuid "64428624-3fb4-579f-a7fa-f7a2a1ff7711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c6c5a6f-f486-57c2-a5c5-e573d7067374" :question/math-academy-id "q-135300" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](images/37/3775fdcb48fd015097b484b6d5ebaefcecd4ef10298cb18b34226a8eae1be1ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196d1c20-1305-5f81-a984-4e2fc6ded6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266352} {:answer/id #uuid "472a0bb2-8925-5776-8dbe-90c5f4e739ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266777} {:answer/id #uuid "74f779d8-62a1-57c6-a0f5-7e10d8eb5f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267171} {:answer/id #uuid "802b5a5d-5d80-5444-8a81-e3bfa0e3e8a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267273} {:answer/id #uuid "b85277e9-8eae-5d51-9439-a4ac0cab536e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A paraboloid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802b5a5d-5d80-5444-8a81-e3bfa0e3e8a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267273} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df87ca7-76d5-574c-a6b2-0660a1d8d605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6e0d64d-6adc-551a-b870-a3578b510cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-135302" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/00/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08cc1fc9-61ac-5985-8f64-58da4f35ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266224} {:answer/id #uuid "58939d3a-1b18-5461-ba93-b671b729d8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266936} {:answer/id #uuid "e652bd2c-6d8c-5ba6-826a-b7fa9c10673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268186} {:answer/id #uuid "e9768716-f4a2-5c1e-b63c-0b7b884e5909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268221} {:answer/id #uuid "ec616c23-9f10-552e-b69e-3ca66e74c9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e652bd2c-6d8c-5ba6-826a-b7fa9c10673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268186} :answer-field/id #uuid "a88266da-0d3f-56f5-89ed-ba11cda724aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a2bbd2-2dd6-5b47-be2c-faba4acc741a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135303" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =0$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =0\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $0.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/83/832655d3a5ee1429fb1e1c005faa8c19d2a92ecfcf00cef64cfb165bf4755729.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d3fc0b9-50bf-591f-bc18-95fb8263002b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 1, 0 \\le θ \\le π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le 2 π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174635} {:answer/id #uuid "bf32d744-f088-59a1-8511-38f930e296f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 5, 0 \\le θ \\le 2 π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174636} {:answer/id #uuid "d607a8b8-911b-5c65-917d-f3c144623457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 5, 0 \\le θ \\le π / 2, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174637} {:answer/id #uuid "1668a2c9-eb04-5250-a5ce-6d864fb66dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 5, 0 \\le θ \\le π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174638} {:answer/id #uuid "5d15c416-0a1c-596a-a0ef-8a26a89330d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 1, 0 \\le θ \\le π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π / 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174639}] :answer-field/id #uuid "169d7945-8e3f-5541-b2e1-e329f344436c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548516a9-ad9f-57e9-a033-d17809986b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371969} {:answer/id #uuid "b82bd607-53d5-56ef-be0b-8c32da4ab71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371970} {:answer/id #uuid "64c45675-1256-533f-9993-66cb1eaca8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371971} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d11e71-624f-5d42-b0d4-35656fd37c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371972} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc50b75-dae8-5026-be62-d0554770a166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c45675-1256-533f-9993-66cb1eaca8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371971} :answer-field/id #uuid "b21e68ec-d30d-5b80-bad7-bc3780fa39d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3cde71-1b72-5270-99f3-cec1724b7c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161732" :question/problem "![](images/33/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e5/e5371e711515feccdaab3f6b10734fb35bb59c724fc408c8b67b4b54132136ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8daf1703-a2fb-5dc3-97cd-c370676d0ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 4 sin ⁡ θ sin ⁡ ϕ, 0 \\le θ \\le π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174647} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecc514e-5c43-5a3a-a614-fe2d66ebdfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 4 ρ sin ⁡ θ sin ⁡ ϕ, 0 \\le θ \\le 2 π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174648} {:answer/id #uuid "9b177d00-7134-5c55-a158-b9bb0120705f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 2 ρ sin ⁡ θ, 0 \\le θ \\le π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174649} {:answer/id #uuid "be182123-fa0b-53e1-bf51-cca9dc58c242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 2 ρ sin ⁡ θ, 0 \\le θ \\le 2 π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174650} {:answer/id #uuid "3766496b-1410-5fcc-9596-f1a4103632a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(ρ, θ, ϕ): 0 \\le ρ \\le 4, 0 \\le θ \\le π, 0 \\le ϕ \\le π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174651}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d54d72eb-3785-5ffb-a661-890ae52834ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74af2fbc-b192-5afc-8573-9975539886f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363432} {:answer/id #uuid "3befc860-c90d-5f94-b541-993433cab936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363433} {:answer/id #uuid "03ac352a-b86e-59ba-81b0-d8afac5cbcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363434} {:answer/id #uuid "086387c4-64c7-55a5-88d0-bccf7e2c931c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363435} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc61c0a-1db1-5dd9-8686-470312795e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74af2fbc-b192-5afc-8573-9975539886f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363432} :answer-field/id #uuid "acc82db6-7cde-56cc-80d8-b767a5862ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f229d07-8a86-5591-a00d-4cf4336bfb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-161736" :question/problem "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a6985012edbaf28ca42d88fd47e3769fa53704cad8f8c09021c944d966a3d48.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56040a8a-d841-5709-a343-73c83bbd44a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279378} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab9053a-bc63-5aae-8359-83c3482b07d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279685} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeafae1-4aea-564c-83e4-050ca4f734e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279687} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed7efad-761a-57ad-a958-cc83c7d4b81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280003} {:answer/id #uuid "b82ce66c-b282-5f4d-9f1d-f7e0bf32b929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56040a8a-d841-5709-a343-73c83bbd44a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279378} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7ce485-b829-5a13-88f2-198fc5306c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a536dd94-d69c-5fc7-af99-867a1849acf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161821" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd321d5afaa151a8da5caa6f4ca561f93566e35ff87235c06854b56cf52c0c69.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^